==Old English==

===Alternative forms===
* {{alter|ang|ih|iċċ}}

===Etymology===
From {{inh|ang|gmw-pro|*ik}}, from {{inh|ang|gem-pro|[[*ek]], [[*ik]]}}, from {{inh|ang|ine-pro|*éǵh₂}}. Germanic cognates include {{cog|ofs|ik}}, {{cog|osx|ik}}, {{cog|odt|ik}} ({{cog|nl|ik}}), {{cog|goh|ih}} ({{cog|de|ich}}), {{cog|non|ek}} ({{cog|sv|jag}}), {{cog|got|𐌹𐌺}}. The Indo-European root, in various forms, is also the source of {{cog|sa|अहम्|tr=ahám}}, {{cog|la|egō}} ({{cog|fr|je}}, {{cog|es|yo}}, {{cog|it|io}} etc.), {{cog|grc|ἐγώ}}, {{cog|lt|aš}}, {{cog|lv|es}}, {{cog|ae|𐬀𐬰𐬆𐬨}}, {{cog|cu|азъ}} ({{cog|ru|я}}, {{cog|bg|аз}}), {{cog|xcl|ես}}. For declined derivations, see under {{m|ang|mē}}, {{m|ang|wē}} etc.

===Pronunciation===
* {{ang-IPA|iċ}}

===Pronoun===
{{head|ang|pronoun|cat2=personal pronouns|head=iċ}}

# [[I]]
#: {{ux|ang|'''Iċ''' lufiġe þē.|'''I''' love you.}}
#* '''c. 990''', ''{{w|Wessex Gospels}}'', [https://archive.org/stream/dahalgangodspelo00thor#page/n203/mode/2up John 6:20]
#*: {{quote|ang|'''''Iċ''' hit eom. Ne ondrǣdaþ ēow.''|It's '''me''' [literally '''''I''' am it'']. Don't be scared.}}
#* ''[https://books.google.com/books?id=i_FBtx0ZPPMC&pg=PA12 The Life of Saint Margaret]''
#*: {{quote|ang|'''''Iċ''' nylle nān word mā of þīnum mūðe ġehīeran.''|'''I''' don't want to hear one more word out of your mouth.}}

====Usage notes====
In modern English, object pronouns are often used as subjects in a wide variety of circumstances ("<u>Me</u> and <u>her</u> are friends", "you're as big as <u>me</u>"). In Old English only subject pronouns were used as subjects (except with a small class of verbs such as {{m|ang|līcian}}, {{m|ang|mǣtan}}, and {{m|ang|twēoġan}}, which took dative or accusative subjects with nouns and pronouns alike). Thus "me and her are friends" was ''<u>Iċ</u> and <u>hēo</u> sind frīend'', literally "<u>I</u> and <u>she</u> are friends." Other examples: ''Þū eart swā miċel swā <u>iċ</u>!'' ("You're as big as <u>me</u>!", lit. "as <u>I</u>"), ''Ġē dōþ simle swelċe ġē beteran bēon þonne <u>iċ</u>'' ("You guys always act like you're better than <u>me</u>", lit. "<u>I</u>"), ''<u>Iċ</u> hit eom'' ("It's <u>me</u>", lit. "<u>I</u> it am").

